INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'What is Model Context Protocol (MCP)?', E'Been working with APIs for over a decade and MCP is genuinely different from anything I''ve seen before. Let me break it down.\n\nModel Context Protocol is a standard that lets AI models connect to external tools and services through a unified interface. Instead of writing custom integration code for every API, you define your tools once and any MCP-compatible AI client can use them.\n\nThe architecture is straightforward:\n- **MCP Server** — exposes tools (functions the AI can call)\n- **MCP Client** — the AI application (Claude Desktop, Cursor, etc.)\n- **Transport** — stdio for local, HTTP for remote\n\n```json\n{\n  "name": "stripe_create_customer",\n  "description": "Create a new Stripe customer",\n  "inputSchema": {\n    "type": "object",\n    "properties": {\n      "email": { "type": "string" },\n      "name": { "type": "string" }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe AI sees the tool schema, decides when to call it, and handles the response. No prompt engineering for API calls — the model figures out which tools to use based on the user''s intent.\n\n0nMCP takes this further with 850+ pre-built tools across 53 services. One npm install, one config, and your AI has access to Stripe, Slack, Supabase, GitHub, and dozens more.\n\nWhat makes this different from Langchain or similar frameworks? MCP is a protocol, not a library. Any client that speaks MCP works with any MCP server. That interoperability is the key insight.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [model context protocol](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '1 hour');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'Agentic AI workflow tutorial for beginners', E'Just started learning about agentic AI and I''m trying to understand how it''s different from regular AI chatbots. From what I gather, an "agent" can actually DO things — not just talk about them.\n\nSo I installed 0nMCP and connected it to Claude Desktop. When I asked Claude to "create a Stripe customer for john@example.com," it actually called the Stripe API and created the customer. That blew my mind.\n\nThe workflow concept is where it gets interesting. Instead of one tool call, you can chain multiple steps:\n\n1. User signs up (webhook)\n2. Create Stripe customer\n3. Send welcome email via SendGrid\n4. Add to Slack channel\n5. Log in Supabase\n\nAll of this happens automatically from a single trigger. The AI figures out the order and handles errors.\n\nMy question for experienced folks: what''s the best way to start building these workflows? Should I begin with simple 2-step chains and work up, or dive into the .0n SWITCH file format right away?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'Autonomous AI agents — building your first one', E'Running 20+ client projects, I needed agents that handle repetitive tasks without me babysitting them. Here''s what actually works in production.\n\nAn autonomous agent needs three things:\n1. **Tools** — APIs it can call (0nMCP handles this)\n2. **Memory** — context about what it''s done (conversation history + .0n files)\n3. **Decision logic** — when to act vs. when to ask for help\n\nThe mistake most people make is going full autonomous too fast. Start with "supervised autonomy" — the agent does the work but you approve actions above a certain threshold.\n\nFor my agency, I set it up so routine tasks (updating CRM contacts, sending scheduled emails, syncing data) run fully autonomous. Anything involving money (Stripe charges, invoice creation) requires a human confirm step.\n\nThe ROI is real: we went from 6 hours/week on data entry to basically zero. That''s across all clients.\n\nAnyone else running agents in production? What guardrails do you use?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [autonomous AI agents](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '5 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', 'MCP vs traditional API integration', E'Security angle on why MCP matters: traditional integrations scatter API keys across dozens of .env files, webhook endpoints, and middleware layers. Each one is an attack surface.\n\nMCP centralizes this. One server holds the credentials, one protocol handles auth, and the AI client never sees raw API keys.\n\n0nMCP''s Vault takes this further with AES-256-GCM encryption and hardware fingerprint binding. Your credentials are encrypted at rest AND tied to your specific machine.\n\nCompare that to a typical Node.js project:\n```\n# Traditional — keys everywhere\nSTRIPE_KEY=sk_live_xxx\nSLACK_TOKEN=xoxb-xxx\nSUPABASE_KEY=eyJhbG...\nSENDGRID_KEY=SG.xxx\n```\n\nvs.\n```bash\n# MCP — one encrypted vault\n0nmcp vault seal ~/.0n/connections/\n```\n\nThe credential surface area drops from N files to 1 encrypted container. For compliance-heavy industries (healthcare, finance), this is a massive win.\n\nThoughts on the security model? I''ve been auditing the Vault source and it looks solid but I want to hear from others.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '7 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'Agentic AI tools comparison 2026', E'Coming from the enterprise data world (Airflow, dbt, Prefect), I''ve been evaluating agentic AI tools. Here''s my honest comparison.\n\n**0nMCP** — 850+ tools, MCP protocol native, CLI-first. Strongest for multi-service orchestration. The .0n config format is clean. Vault encryption is enterprise-grade.\n\n**Langchain** — Python-heavy, lots of abstractions. Good for LLM chains but the API integration story is bolted on. Too many breaking changes between versions.\n\n**CrewAI** — Multi-agent focused. Good for agent-to-agent delegation but limited tool ecosystem. You end up writing custom tools for everything.\n\n**AutoGPT/AgentGPT** — Impressive demos, struggles in production. No standardized tool protocol.\n\n**n8n/Make** — Visual workflow builders. Great for non-developers. But they''re not AI-native — you''re still manually wiring every connection.\n\nThe differentiator for 0nMCP is the protocol layer. MCP means your tools work with Claude, Cursor, Windsurf, or any future MCP client. That''s not vendor lock-in — it''s an open standard.\n\nWhat tools are you all using? Curious about real production experiences.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [generative AI tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '9 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'How I replaced 3 SaaS tools with one MCP server', E'Budget is always tight when you''re freelancing. I was paying $50/month for Zapier, $30 for a form handler, and $20 for an email scheduler. $100/month just to glue things together.\n\nThen I set up 0nMCP. One npm install, connected my existing Stripe/SendGrid/Supabase accounts, and built the same automations as .0n SWITCH files. Total cost: $0 extra (I already had those API accounts).\n\nThe webhook handler alone replaced Zapier for my use case — Stripe payment comes in, customer gets added to Supabase, welcome email fires via SendGrid, Slack notification to me. All in one .0n file.\n\nTook me a Saturday afternoon to set up. If you''re paying for integration middleware and you can write basic config files, seriously look at this.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '11 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'Best practices for AI API orchestration', E'After building orchestration systems for years, here are patterns that actually hold up in production.\n\n**1. Rate limit everything.** Every external API has limits. 0nMCP''s token bucket system handles this per-service with exponential backoff. Don''t roll your own.\n\n**2. Idempotency keys.** When a workflow step fails mid-execution, you need to retry safely. Always include idempotency keys for payment and mutation operations.\n\n**3. Circuit breakers.** If Stripe is down, don''t keep hammering it. Fail fast and queue for retry.\n\n```javascript\n// 0nMCP handles this internally\n// But if building custom tools:\nconst result = await rateLimiter.execute(''stripe'', async () => {\n  return stripe.customers.create({ email })\n})\n```\n\n**4. Separate read and write paths.** Reads can be cached aggressively. Writes need confirmation flows.\n\n**5. Log everything.** The .0n history directory (`~/.0n/history/`) gives you JSONL execution logs. Use them.\n\nWhat patterns have you found essential? Curious about edge cases others have hit.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '13 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'Is agentic AI actually useful or just hype?', E'Honest question — I keep seeing "agentic AI" everywhere and I can''t tell if it''s a real paradigm shift or just marketing.\n\nFrom what I''ve tested so far, the difference between a chatbot and an agent is that agents can take actions. Claude without MCP just tells you what to do. Claude WITH MCP actually does it.\n\nBut is that enough to call it a new era? Or is it just function calling with extra steps?\n\nI''d love to hear from people who''ve been in tech longer than me. When you first saw APIs, containers, or serverless — did it feel like this? Is this the same kind of inflection point?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '15 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'The business case for MCP adoption', E'Just pitched MCP to a client''s CTO and won the deal. Here''s the deck in text form.\n\n**Problem:** Client has 12 SaaS tools, 3 developers spending 40% of time on integration maintenance. Zapier bill is $500/month and growing.\n\n**Solution:** 0nMCP as the universal integration layer. One server, all tools accessible to their AI assistant.\n\n**Numbers:**\n- Integration maintenance: 40% → 5% of dev time\n- Monthly SaaS savings: $500 → $0 (direct API calls)\n- New automation deployment: 2 weeks → 2 hours\n- Total first-year savings: ~$180K\n\nThe CTO''s biggest concern was vendor lock-in. MCP being an open protocol was the clincher — if something better comes along, their tool definitions are portable.\n\nAnyone else selling MCP adoption to enterprises? What objections are you hearing?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP protocol implementation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '17 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', 'Credential management in multi-agent AI systems', E'When you have multiple agents accessing shared services, credential management gets hairy fast. Here''s the threat model I use.\n\n**Risk 1: Key sprawl.** Each agent needs API keys. More copies = more exposure.\n**Mitigation:** Centralized MCP server. Agents call tools through MCP, never see raw keys.\n\n**Risk 2: Privilege escalation.** An agent meant for read-only Stripe access could theoretically call charge endpoints.\n**Mitigation:** Tool-level permissions. 0nMCP lets you expose only specific tools per client.\n\n**Risk 3: Audit trail gaps.** Which agent made which API call?\n**Mitigation:** 0nMCP''s JSONL history logs every execution with timestamps and inputs.\n\nThe 0nVault Container system (patent pending) adds another layer — Argon2id double-encryption for credentials with hardware fingerprint binding. Even if someone gets the container file, they can''t decrypt it on a different machine.\n\nWhat''s your credential hygiene like? I''d bet most people here have at least one API key in a git commit somewhere.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [multi-agent AI systems](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', now() + interval '19 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'reply', E'The interoperability point is what sold me. I''ve been burned by Langchain breaking changes three times this year. MCP being a protocol means my tool definitions survive client upgrades. That alone justified the switch for our agency.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', 'what-is-model-context-protocol-mcp', now() + interval '2 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'reply', E'From a security standpoint, the fact that the AI client never sees raw API keys is huge. In traditional setups the LLM prompt often contains credentials. MCP keeps the tool execution server-side — the model only gets structured results back.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', 'what-is-model-context-protocol-mcp', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'Start simple, Dev. Seriously. Two-step chains first — webhook trigger → single API call. Get comfortable with how data flows between steps before touching SWITCH files. The .0n format is powerful but you need to understand the basics first.\n\nMy progression was: single tool calls → 2-step chains → conditional logic → full SWITCH files. Took about a week.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', 'agentic-ai-workflow-tutorial-for-beginners', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'reply', E'Was in the exact same spot six months ago. Started with a simple Stripe webhook → Supabase logger. Once that worked, I added SendGrid emails, then Slack notifications. Building incrementally meant I could debug each step individually.\n\nDon''t try to build a 10-step workflow on day one. Trust me on that.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', 'agentic-ai-workflow-tutorial-for-beginners', now() + interval '5 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'reply', E'The supervised autonomy approach Marcus describes maps directly to what we call "human-in-the-loop" in data engineering. Same pattern — automate the boring stuff, require approval for consequential actions.\n\nOne addition: log EVERY autonomous action to an audit table. When something goes wrong at 3am, you need the paper trail.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [autonomous AI agents](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'general', 'autonomous-ai-agents-building-your-first-one', now() + interval '6 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
