INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'Understanding 0nMCP execution patterns: Pipeline vs Assembly Line vs Radial Burst', E'0nMCP has three execution patterns and choosing the right one matters for performance.\n\n**Pipeline** — Sequential. Step 1 completes, output feeds Step 2, and so on. Use when each step depends on the previous result.\n```\nwebhook → create_customer → send_email → log_result\n```\n\n**Assembly Line** — Parallel batches. Independent steps run simultaneously, then converge. Use when multiple independent operations share a trigger.\n```\ntrigger → [create_customer, send_slack, log_event] → summary\n```\n\n**Radial Burst** — Fan-out/fan-in. One input spawns N parallel executions (e.g., process each item in a list), then collects results. Use for batch operations.\n```\nget_contacts → [process_contact_1, process_contact_2, ...N] → aggregate\n```\n\nThe performance difference is significant. A 10-step Pipeline takes 10x single-step latency. An Assembly Line with 10 independent steps takes 1x. Radial Burst processing 100 items is 100x faster than sequential.\n\nWhich patterns are you using most? I find myself defaulting to Pipeline and have to consciously think about parallelization.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '2 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'Real workflow: Client onboarding automation with .0n SWITCH files', E'Sharing an actual production workflow that runs for every new client signup. This handles everything from payment to project setup.\n\nThe SWITCH file chains 7 services:\n1. Stripe webhook fires on `checkout.session.completed`\n2. Create contact in CRM with payment details\n3. Provision Supabase row with client config\n4. Create Slack channel `#client-{name}`\n5. Send welcome email via SendGrid with login credentials\n6. Create GitHub repo from template\n7. Log everything to the audit trail\n\nBefore this workflow, onboarding took 45 minutes of manual setup per client. Now it''s instant.\n\nThe .0n format makes it portable — I can share this workflow with other agency owners and they just swap their API keys. The SWITCH file concept is underrated.\n\nHappy to share the actual .0n file if anyone wants to adapt it.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'Workflow chaining: passing data between steps', E'Figured out something useful about data flow in 0nMCP workflows. The variable resolution system has a clear priority order:\n\n`{{system.*}}` > `{{launch.*}}` > `{{inputs.*}}` > `{{step.output.*}}`\n\nSo if you need to pass the output of Step 1 into Step 3, you reference it as `{{step.1.output.customer_id}}`. The template engine handles deep paths too — `{{step.2.output.data.items[0].name}}`.\n\nTripped me up at first because I was trying to use environment variables for inter-step data. Don''t do that — the template system is purpose-built for this.\n\nAnyone know if there''s a way to do conditional branching? Like "if step 2 returns error, skip step 3 and go to step 4"?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI-powered workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '6 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'Comparing .0n SWITCH files to Airflow DAGs', E'For anyone coming from the data engineering world, .0n SWITCH files are essentially DAGs in a simpler format.\n\n**Airflow DAG:**\n- Python code defining task dependencies\n- Requires Airflow scheduler + webserver + database\n- Great for scheduled batch jobs\n- Heavy infrastructure overhead\n\n**.0n SWITCH file:**\n- JSON/YAML config with step definitions\n- Runs on 0nMCP (single process)\n- Event-driven OR scheduled\n- Zero infrastructure beyond the MCP server\n\nThe trade-off is clear: Airflow gives you industrial-grade scheduling and monitoring. 0nMCP gives you simplicity and AI-native execution. For my use case (real-time API orchestration, not batch ETL), 0nMCP wins easily.\n\nBut I do miss Airflow''s retry policies and SLA monitoring. Would love to see those in 0nMCP eventually.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '8 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'My first .0n SWITCH file — feedback welcome', E'Just wrote my first workflow and would appreciate feedback. It''s a simple contact form handler.\n\n```json\n{\n  "name": "contact-form-handler",\n  "version": "1.0.0",\n  "trigger": {\n    "type": "webhook",\n    "path": "/contact"\n  },\n  "steps": [\n    {\n      "id": "save",\n      "service": "supabase",\n      "action": "insert_row",\n      "inputs": {\n        "table": "contacts",\n        "data": "{{inputs.body}}"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      "id": "notify",\n      "service": "slack",\n      "action": "send_message",\n      "inputs": {\n        "channel": "#leads",\n        "text": "New contact: {{inputs.body.name}} ({{inputs.body.email}})"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nDoes this look right? Not sure if the template syntax for nested body fields is correct. Also wondering if I should add error handling.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '10 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', 'Securing workflow execution: input validation and sanitization', E'Something that bugs me about most workflow tools — they blindly pass user input through every step without validation. That''s how injection attacks happen.\n\nIn 0nMCP, you should validate at the trigger boundary:\n\n1. **Schema validation** — Use Zod or JSON Schema to validate webhook payloads BEFORE they enter the pipeline\n2. **Input sanitization** — Strip HTML/script tags from string inputs\n3. **Rate limiting** — 0nMCP has built-in token bucket rate limiting per service\n4. **Webhook verification** — HMAC signatures for Stripe, GitHub, Slack, etc.\n\nThe webhook verification is already handled by `webhooks.js` in the 0nMCP codebase — it supports Stripe, CRM, Slack, GitHub, Twilio, and Shopify signature verification out of the box.\n\nDon''t skip this. One unvalidated webhook payload that hits a Stripe charge endpoint could cost you real money.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '12 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'Error handling patterns in multi-step workflows', E'Wrote up the error handling patterns I use in production workflows. These have saved me from 3am pages.\n\n**Pattern 1: Compensating transactions.** If Step 3 fails after Steps 1-2 succeeded, run cleanup steps to undo 1-2. Essential for financial operations.\n\n**Pattern 2: Dead letter queue.** Failed items go to a separate queue for manual review. Don''t lose data just because processing failed.\n\n**Pattern 3: Retry with backoff.** 0nMCP''s rate limiter handles this per-service, but you can also add workflow-level retries in your SWITCH file.\n\n**Pattern 4: Circuit breaker.** After N failures to the same service, stop trying for M minutes. Prevents cascade failures.\n\nThe most common mistake: catching errors silently. Always log the full error context — service, inputs, timestamp, and the specific failure reason.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '14 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'AI workflow automation — from zero to production', E'Step-by-step guide for going from "I just installed 0nMCP" to "my workflows run in production."\n\n**Week 1: Foundation**\n- Install 0nMCP globally: `npm i -g 0nmcp`\n- Import your API keys: `0nmcp engine import`\n- Verify connections: `0nmcp engine verify`\n- Make your first tool call through Claude Desktop\n\n**Week 2: First Workflow**\n- Write a 2-step .0n SWITCH file\n- Test locally with `0nmcp run workflow.0n`\n- Add error handling\n\n**Week 3: Production**\n- Start HTTP server: `0nmcp serve`\n- Set up webhook endpoints\n- Add monitoring (check `~/.0n/history/`)\n- Deploy to your server/VPS\n\n**Week 4: Scale**\n- Add parallel execution (Assembly Line pattern)\n- Build reusable workflow templates\n- Share SWITCH files across projects\n\nThis is the progression I took every new team member through. Most people are productive by end of Week 2.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '16 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'Scheduling recurring workflows with 0nMCP', E'Needed a daily workflow that syncs Shopify orders to Supabase and sends a summary to Slack. Here''s how I set up scheduling.\n\n0nMCP''s server mode supports cron-style scheduling through the CronScheduler class. In your config:\n\n```json\n{\n  "schedules": [\n    {\n      "cron": "0 9 * * *",\n      "workflow": "daily-order-sync.0n"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis runs the workflow every day at 9am. The scheduler is part of the Application Engine — you start it with `0nmcp serve` and it handles both HTTP webhooks and cron schedules.\n\nFor my use case, the daily sync pulls all Shopify orders from the last 24h, bulk inserts them into Supabase, calculates totals, and sends a formatted Slack summary. Total execution time: ~4 seconds for ~50 orders.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '18 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'Building multi-agent AI systems with 0nMCP', E'Set up a system where three specialized agents collaborate on client reports. Here''s the architecture.\n\n**Agent 1: Data Collector** — Pulls from Google Analytics, Stripe, and CRM. Outputs raw metrics.\n**Agent 2: Analyzer** — Takes raw metrics, runs statistical analysis, identifies trends.\n**Agent 3: Writer** — Takes analysis, generates client-facing report in markdown.\n\nEach agent is a separate MCP client connected to the same 0nMCP server. They communicate through a shared Supabase table (agent_messages). The orchestrator workflow kicks off Agent 1, waits for completion, triggers Agent 2, then Agent 3.\n\nTotal report generation time: ~45 seconds. Previously this was a 3-hour manual process every Monday.\n\nThe key insight: each agent only has access to the tools it needs. The writer can''t accidentally modify Stripe data.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [multi-agent AI systems](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', now() + interval '20 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'reply', E'Radial Burst is a game changer for bulk operations. We use it to process monthly invoices — 200+ clients, each needing a personalized invoice generated and emailed. Sequential would take 30 minutes. Radial Burst does it in under 2 minutes.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', 'understanding-0nmcp-execution-patterns-pipeline-vs-assembly-line-vs-radial-burst', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'reply', E'This execution pattern breakdown is exactly what I needed. Been running everything as Pipeline without realizing I could parallelize the independent steps. Refactoring my contact form handler to use Assembly Line for the save + notify steps — they don''t depend on each other.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI-powered workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', 'understanding-0nmcp-execution-patterns-pipeline-vs-assembly-line-vs-radial-burst', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'The SWITCH file looks solid, Dev. Two suggestions:\n1. Wrap `inputs.body` in explicit field references: `{{inputs.body.name}}`, `{{inputs.body.email}}` — avoids passing the raw object\n2. Add a `depends_on` field to the notify step referencing the save step, so notification only fires after successful save\n\nOtherwise the structure is exactly right.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', 'my-first-0n-switch-file-feedback-welcome', now() + interval '11 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'reply', E'Add input validation before the save step. Right now any payload hits your Supabase table directly. At minimum, validate that `name` and `email` exist and `email` matches a basic pattern. One malformed request could insert garbage data.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', 'my-first-0n-switch-file-feedback-welcome', now() + interval '12 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'reply', E'Yes — conditional branching works with the `condition` internal action. Something like:\n```json\n{\n  "id": "check",\n  "action": "condition",\n  "inputs": {\n    "expression": "{{step.2.output.status}} === ''error''",\n    "true_step": "step_4",\n    "false_step": "step_3"\n  }\n}\n```\nBeen using this for payment processing — skip invoice generation if the charge fails.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', 'workflow-chaining-passing-data-between-steps', now() + interval '7 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'reply', E'The compensating transaction pattern maps directly to saga patterns in distributed systems. One thing I''d add: always log the compensation attempt too. If your undo step also fails, you need to know about it immediately.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'workflows', 'error-handling-patterns-in-multi-step-workflows', now() + interval '15 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'How to set up an MCP server with Claude Desktop', E'Took me a bit to figure this out so documenting for others. Here''s the step-by-step.\n\n**1. Install 0nMCP:**\n```bash\nnpm install -g 0nmcp\n```\n\n**2. Import your API keys:**\n```bash\n0nmcp engine import\n```\nThis scans your .env files and auto-maps credentials to services.\n\n**3. Configure Claude Desktop:**\nEdit `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`:\n```json\n{\n  "mcpServers": {\n    "0nMCP": {\n      "command": "node",\n      "args": ["/path/to/0nMCP/index.js"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**4. Restart Claude Desktop.** You should see the tools icon (hammer) in the input area.\n\n**5. Test it:** Ask Claude "List my Stripe customers" — it should call the 0nMCP tool and return results.\n\nThe gotcha that tripped me up: the path in args needs to be absolute, not relative. Also make sure your API keys are imported before configuring Claude.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'How to automate Stripe + Shopify with AI', E'This combo runs our entire e-commerce backend. When a Shopify order comes in, we need to:\n\n1. Verify payment in Stripe\n2. Update inventory in Shopify\n3. Create shipping label\n4. Send confirmation email\n5. Update CRM contact\n\nWith 0nMCP, this is one workflow triggered by a Shopify webhook. The AI handles the orchestration — it knows which service to call for each step and how to pass data between them.\n\nThe Stripe + Shopify integration is particularly smooth because 0nMCP has 35+ Shopify tools and 16+ Stripe tools pre-built. No custom API wrappers needed.\n\nBiggest win: returns and refunds. When a customer requests a refund, the agent automatically processes the Stripe refund, restocks the Shopify item, and sends an apology email. Used to take 15 minutes per case.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI-powered workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '5 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'Supabase + 0nMCP: real-time data pipelines', E'Built a real-time analytics pipeline using Supabase and 0nMCP that''s surprisingly elegant.\n\nThe architecture:\n- Supabase Realtime listens for new rows in `events` table\n- Webhook fires to 0nMCP HTTP server\n- 0nMCP workflow processes the event: enriches data, computes aggregates, writes back to Supabase\n- Frontend sees updated dashboards via Supabase Realtime subscriptions\n\nLatency from event insertion to dashboard update: ~800ms. For a system with zero dedicated infrastructure beyond Supabase and an MCP server, that''s remarkable.\n\nThe 0nMCP Supabase tools handle CRUD, RPC calls, storage, and even realtime subscriptions. Having all of that accessible through MCP means the AI can build and modify these pipelines conversationally.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '7 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'Slack + Discord integration: cross-platform notifications', E'Set up a notification system that mirrors alerts between Slack and Discord. My clients use both and I was tired of posting updates in two places.\n\n0nMCP has 35 social tools that cover Slack and Discord. The workflow:\n1. New message in Slack `#updates` channel (webhook)\n2. Format message for Discord (strip Slack-specific markup)\n3. Post to Discord `#updates` channel\n4. Works in reverse too — Discord webhook → format → Slack\n\nThe formatting step is key — Slack and Discord markdown are slightly different. The AI handles the translation automatically when you describe the intent.\n\nTotal setup time: 20 minutes. Saved me from manually cross-posting 5-10 updates per day.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '9 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', 'GitHub + 0nMCP: automated security workflows', E'Built a security pipeline that monitors GitHub repos for vulnerabilities and takes action.\n\nThe workflow triggers on GitHub webhooks:\n- **Push event** → scan for leaked credentials (regex patterns for API keys, passwords)\n- **Dependabot alert** → create Slack notification + JIRA ticket\n- **PR opened** → check for security anti-patterns\n\nThe credential scanner is particularly useful. It checks for patterns like `sk_live_`, `AKIA`, `ghp_`, and other API key formats. If found, it immediately creates a Slack alert with the commit SHA and file path.\n\n0nMCP''s GitHub tools handle repo, issue, and PR operations. Combined with Slack and JIRA tools, the entire security triage process is automated.\n\nFalse positive rate is about 5% — mostly from test fixtures and documentation examples. Added an allowlist for known test files.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP protocol implementation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '11 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'MongoDB + Supabase sync via 0nMCP', E'Needed to keep a MongoDB collection in sync with Supabase tables for a migration project. 0nMCP made this straightforward.\n\nThe sync workflow:\n1. MongoDB change stream fires on insert/update/delete\n2. 0nMCP webhook receives the change event\n3. Transform document to relational format\n4. Upsert into Supabase table\n\nHandled ~10K documents in the initial sync. Ongoing changes sync in near-real-time.\n\nThe transformation step is where the AI shines — MongoDB documents are nested and schemaless. The AI flattens them into appropriate Supabase columns based on the target schema. I described the mapping once and it handles edge cases automatically.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [generative AI tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '13 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'CRM + email automation with 0nMCP', E'We manage CRM contacts and email campaigns for 20+ clients. The manual process was killing us — update contact, tag them, trigger email sequence, log activity. Repeat 50 times a day.\n\n0nMCP''s CRM module has 245 tools covering contacts, conversations, calendars, opportunities, invoices, social, and more. Combined with SendGrid for transactional email, the automation handles:\n\n- New lead → CRM contact created → tagged by source → welcome email sent\n- Contact replies → conversation logged → notification to assigned rep\n- Opportunity status change → update invoice → notify finance team\n\nThe CRM tools use a data-driven factory pattern so they''re consistent and reliable. Each tool maps directly to an API endpoint with proper validation.\n\nCut our CRM management time by 70% across all client accounts.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '15 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'Connecting Notion + Google Sheets via MCP', E'My professor wants class data flowing between Notion (project management) and Google Sheets (grade tracking). Figured 0nMCP could handle this.\n\nThe setup:\n- Notion database has student projects with status, deadline, and grade fields\n- Google Sheet has the master gradebook\n- 0nMCP syncs between them bidirectionally\n\nWhen a project status changes in Notion → update the corresponding row in Google Sheets. When a grade is entered in Google Sheets → update the Notion page.\n\n0nMCP has tools for both Notion and Google Sheets. The tricky part was mapping Notion''s page properties to spreadsheet columns. Used the template engine to handle the transformation.\n\nNot the most complex use case but it''s my first real integration and it actually works.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [model context protocol](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', now() + interval '17 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'Good writeup, Dev. One additional tip: run `0nmcp engine verify` after importing to confirm all keys are valid. Catches typos before you waste time debugging "unauthorized" errors in Claude Desktop.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', 'how-to-set-up-an-mcp-server-with-claude-desktop', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'reply', E'The Stripe + Shopify refund automation alone would save me hours per week. Currently processing returns manually because I was worried about automated refunds going wrong. But with the supervised autonomy approach, you could require approval for refunds over a certain amount.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', 'how-to-automate-stripe-shopify-with-ai', now() + interval '6 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'reply', E'800ms latency is impressive but make sure you''re not exposing the Supabase service role key in the webhook endpoint. Use the anon key for client-side operations and only escalate to service role for server-side processing within the MCP server.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', 'supabase-0nmcp-real-time-data-pipelines', now() + interval '8 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'reply', E'The credential scanning workflow is exactly what we needed. One of our junior devs accidentally committed a Stripe test key last month. Having automated detection would have caught it in seconds instead of hours.\n\nDoes the allowlist support regex patterns or just exact file paths?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [autonomous AI agents](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', 'github-0nmcp-automated-security-workflows', now() + interval '12 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'reply', E'Notion → Google Sheets is a great starter integration. The property mapping can get complex with Notion''s relation and rollup fields — if you hit those, let me know. I built a transformer for nested Notion data that might help.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [generative AI tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'integrations', 'connecting-notion-google-sheets-via-mcp', now() + interval '18 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'Model context protocol tutorial: from install to first tool call', E'Complete tutorial for getting started with MCP and 0nMCP. Follow along step by step.\n\n**Prerequisites:** Node.js 18+, an API key for at least one service (Stripe, Slack, etc.)\n\n**Step 1: Install**\n```bash\nnpm install -g 0nmcp\n```\n\n**Step 2: Set up credentials**\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.0n/connections\n0nmcp engine import  # auto-detects .env files\n```\n\n**Step 3: Verify**\n```bash\n0nmcp engine verify\n# Should show green checkmarks for each connected service\n```\n\n**Step 4: Start the MCP server**\n```bash\n0nmcp  # stdio mode for Claude Desktop\n# or\n0nmcp serve --port 3001  # HTTP mode for web apps\n```\n\n**Step 5: Make your first tool call**\nIn Claude Desktop (with MCP configured), type:\n"List my recent Stripe charges"\n\nClaude will call the `stripe_list_charges` tool and return formatted results.\n\n**Step 6: Build a workflow**\nCreate `hello.0n`:\n```json\n{\n  "name": "hello-world",\n  "steps": [{\n    "service": "slack",\n    "action": "send_message",\n    "inputs": { "channel": "#general", "text": "Hello from 0nMCP!" }\n  }]\n}\n```\n\nRun it: `0nmcp run hello.0n`\n\nThat''s the foundation. From here you can add steps, chain workflows, and build automations.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [model context protocol](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '2 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', '0nMCP Vault walkthrough: encrypting your API credentials', E'Your API keys are the keys to your kingdom. Here''s how to lock them down with 0nMCP Vault.\n\n**What Vault does:** AES-256-GCM encryption with PBKDF2-SHA512 key derivation (100K iterations) + hardware fingerprint binding.\n\n**Seal your credentials:**\n```bash\n0nmcp vault seal\n# Encrypts all files in ~/.0n/connections/\n# Prompts for a passphrase\n```\n\n**Unseal when needed:**\n```bash\n0nmcp vault unseal\n# Decrypts in-memory for the current session\n```\n\n**Verify integrity:**\n```bash\n0nmcp vault verify\n# Checks that files haven''t been tampered with\n```\n\n**Check your fingerprint:**\n```bash\n0nmcp vault fingerprint\n# Shows the hardware-derived fingerprint\n```\n\nThe hardware fingerprint means even if someone copies your encrypted vault file, they can''t decrypt it on a different machine. This is particularly important for shared CI/CD environments — don''t put vault files in Docker images.\n\nFor portable encryption (works on any machine), use the Engine module instead: `0nmcp engine export`. It uses passphrase-only AES-256-GCM without the fingerprint binding.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'Turn it 0n: importing credentials from .env files', E'The "Turn it 0n" process is how you get your existing API keys into 0nMCP. Took me 5 minutes.\n\n```bash\n0nmcp engine import\n```\n\nThis scans for `.env`, `.env.local`, and similar files in your current directory and home folder. It auto-maps keys to the right service:\n- `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` → Stripe service\n- `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` → Slack service\n- `SUPABASE_URL` + `SUPABASE_KEY` → Supabase service\n- etc.\n\nSupports 53 services total. If a key doesn''t auto-map, you can manually assign it.\n\nAfter import, verify everything works:\n```bash\n0nmcp engine verify\n```\n\nThis actually tests each API key by making a lightweight call to each service. Way better than finding out your key expired when a workflow fails at 2am.\n\nPro tip: run `0nmcp engine platforms` to generate ready-to-use config for Claude Desktop, Cursor, Windsurf, and other MCP clients.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '6 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'Building a data pipeline with 0nMCP: complete walkthrough', E'Step-by-step guide for building a data pipeline that collects, transforms, and loads data using 0nMCP.\n\n**Use case:** Daily sync of Google Sheets data → transform → load into Supabase.\n\n**Step 1: Read from Google Sheets**\nTool: `google_sheets_read_spreadsheet`\nInput: spreadsheet ID, range (e.g., "Sheet1!A1:Z100")\n\n**Step 2: Transform data**\nUse the `transform` internal action in your .0n file:\n```json\n{\n  "id": "transform",\n  "action": "transform",\n  "inputs": {\n    "data": "{{step.1.output.values}}",\n    "operations": [\n      { "type": "rename", "from": "Company Name", "to": "company_name" },\n      { "type": "filter", "field": "status", "value": "active" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Step 3: Load into Supabase**\nTool: `supabase_upsert_rows`\nInput: table name, rows from transform output\n\n**Step 4: Log results**\nTool: `slack_send_message`\nInput: summary of rows synced\n\nThe transform step is where 0nMCP''s template engine shines. You can do field mapping, filtering, and computation without writing code.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI-powered workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '8 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', '0nMCP CLI basics: the commands I use every day', E'Been using 0nMCP for a few weeks now. Here are the CLI commands I actually use daily.\n\n**Starting up:**\n```bash\n0nmcp                    # Start MCP server (stdio mode)\n0nmcp serve --port 3001  # Start HTTP server\n```\n\n**Running workflows:**\n```bash\n0nmcp run my-workflow.0n              # Run a workflow\n0nmcp run my-workflow.0n --input key=value  # With input variables\n```\n\n**Credential management:**\n```bash\n0nmcp engine import    # Import from .env files\n0nmcp engine verify    # Test all API keys\n0nmcp engine platforms # Generate AI client configs\n```\n\n**Vault operations:**\n```bash\n0nmcp vault seal       # Encrypt credentials\n0nmcp vault unseal     # Decrypt for session\n```\n\nThe one I keep forgetting: `0nmcp engine platforms` generates the JSON config for Claude Desktop, Cursor, etc. Saves me from manually editing config files every time.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [model context protocol](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '10 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'Writing custom MCP tools for 0nMCP', E'Sometimes the built-in 850+ tools aren''t enough. Here''s how to add custom tools.\n\n0nMCP uses the `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` for tool registration. The pattern:\n\n```javascript\nimport { McpServer } from ''@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js''\n\nconst server = new McpServer({ name: ''my-tools'' })\n\nserver.tool(\n  ''my_custom_tool'',\n  ''Description of what it does'',\n  {\n    param1: { type: ''string'', description: ''First parameter'' },\n    param2: { type: ''number'', description: ''Second parameter'' },\n  },\n  async ({ param1, param2 }) => {\n    // Your logic here\n    const result = await doSomething(param1, param2)\n    return { content: [{ type: ''text'', text: JSON.stringify(result) }] }\n  }\n)\n```\n\nThe key things:\n1. Tool name should be `snake_case`\n2. Description should be clear — the AI reads it to decide when to use the tool\n3. Input schema uses JSON Schema format\n4. Return format must be `{ content: [{ type: ''text'', text: string }] }`\n\nFor adding tools to 0nMCP''s catalog, follow the data-driven pattern in `catalog.js` — define the service config and `registerTools()` handles the rest.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP protocol implementation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '12 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'Setting up webhook handlers in 0nMCP', E'Webhooks are the backbone of real-time automation. Here''s how to set them up in 0nMCP.\n\n**1. Start the HTTP server:**\n```bash\n0nmcp serve --port 3001\n```\n\n**2. Register webhook endpoints in your workflow:**\nThe server automatically creates endpoints at `/webhook/{service}` for each configured service.\n\n**3. Configure the source service:**\n- Stripe: Add `https://your-domain.com/webhook/stripe` in Stripe Dashboard → Webhooks\n- GitHub: Add webhook URL in repo settings\n- Shopify: Add in app settings → Notifications\n\n**4. Webhook verification:**\n0nMCP auto-verifies signatures for Stripe, GitHub, Slack, Twilio, Shopify, and CRM webhooks. No manual HMAC code needed.\n\n**5. Handle the event:**\nYour .0n workflow receives the webhook payload as `{{inputs.body}}` and the headers as `{{inputs.headers}}`.\n\nPro tip: use `0nmcp serve` in development and a reverse proxy (nginx, Cloudflare Tunnel) in production. Never expose the MCP server directly to the internet.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '14 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', '0nVault Container system: packaging business assets securely', E'The 0nVault Container system (patent pending) lets you package entire business digital assets into a single encrypted file.\n\n**What goes in a container:**\n- Workflows (.0n SWITCH files)\n- Credentials (double-encrypted with Argon2id)\n- Environment variables\n- MCP configurations\n- Site profiles\n- AI brain data\n- Audit trails\n\n**Creating a container:**\n```bash\n0nmcp vault create --name my-business --layers workflows,credentials,env_vars\n```\n\n**Key security features:**\n- 7 semantic layers, each independently encrypted\n- Credentials use Argon2id double-encryption\n- Seal of Truth: SHA3-256 integrity verification\n- Ed25519 digital signatures\n- Multi-party escrow with X25519 ECDH\n- Transfer registry with replay prevention\n\nThe multi-party escrow is the killer feature for agencies. You can grant a client access to specific layers (e.g., workflows but not credentials) while keeping full control. Up to 8 parties per escrow.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [autonomous AI agents](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', now() + interval '16 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'reply', E'This tutorial is exactly what I needed when I started. The `engine verify` step saved me from debugging a typo in my Stripe key for an hour. Should be step 1 in everyone''s setup process.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [model context protocol](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', 'model-context-protocol-tutorial-from-install-to-first-tool-call', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'reply', E'Solid walkthrough, Priya. For agencies managing multiple client environments, I''d add: use separate .0n connection directories per client. The `--config` flag lets you point to different credential sets without mixing them up.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', 'model-context-protocol-tutorial-from-install-to-first-tool-call', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'The portable vs machine-bound encryption distinction is important and often missed. Use Vault (machine-bound) for your development machine. Use Engine export (portable) when sharing credentials with a team or deploying to servers. Different threat models, different tools.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', '0nmcp-vault-walkthrough-encrypting-your-api-credentials', now() + interval '5 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'reply', E'The auto-mapping for credentials is clever. It recognized my BigQuery service account key from the JSON structure, not just the env var name. That''s a nice touch for people who don''t follow standard naming conventions.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [generative AI tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', 'turn-it-0n-importing-credentials-from-env-files', now() + interval '7 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'reply', E'The data-driven pattern for custom tools is clean. I added a custom tool for a niche invoicing API my client uses — took about 15 minutes following the catalog.js pattern. The `registerTools()` function handles all the MCP boilerplate.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'tutorials', 'writing-custom-mcp-tools-for-0nmcp', now() + interval '13 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'Results: 70% reduction in client onboarding time', E'Sharing real numbers from our agency after 3 months of 0nMCP adoption.\n\n**Before (manual process):**\n- Client onboarding: 3 hours/client\n- Weekly reporting: 5 hours\n- CRM data entry: 6 hours/week\n- Invoice generation: 2 hours/month\n- Total: ~15 hours/week on operations\n\n**After (0nMCP automation):**\n- Client onboarding: 15 minutes/client (webhook-triggered)\n- Weekly reporting: automated (0nMCP + Claude generates reports)\n- CRM data entry: eliminated (real-time sync)\n- Invoice generation: automated (Stripe → CRM → email)\n- Total: ~4 hours/week on operations\n\nThat''s an 11-hour/week savings. At our billing rate, that''s $8,800/month in recovered capacity.\n\nThe onboarding workflow alone handles: Stripe subscription setup → CRM contact creation → Slack channel creation → welcome email → GitHub repo provisioning → Supabase project setup.\n\nHappy to detail any specific workflow for anyone interested.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'Freelancer automation stack: $0 infrastructure cost', E'Sharing my setup because it might help other solo operators.\n\n**Stack:**\n- 0nMCP (free, open source)\n- Supabase (free tier — 500MB, 50K rows)\n- Stripe (pay per transaction)\n- SendGrid (free tier — 100 emails/day)\n- Vercel (free tier — hobby project)\n\n**Monthly cost: $0** (all within free tiers)\n\n**What it automates:**\n- Client invoicing (Stripe → email)\n- Project status updates (Supabase → Slack DM)\n- Lead capture (website form → Supabase → email notification)\n- Time tracking summaries (manual input → formatted report)\n\nThe key insight: 0nMCP is the glue. Each individual service has a free tier, and 0nMCP connects them without needing a paid middleware like Zapier.\n\nFor freelancers watching every dollar, this is the way.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', now() + interval '5 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'Showcase: automated data quality pipeline', E'Built a data quality monitoring system using 0nMCP that runs daily checks on our Supabase tables.\n\n**What it checks:**\n- Null values in required fields\n- Duplicate entries by email/phone\n- Orphaned references (foreign key integrity)\n- Stale records (no updates in 30+ days)\n- Format validation (email patterns, phone numbers)\n\n**Results per day:**\n- Scans ~50K rows across 12 tables\n- Flags ~20-50 issues on average\n- Auto-fixes simple problems (standardize phone format, trim whitespace)\n- Creates Slack report for manual review items\n\nExecution time: ~12 seconds. Previously this was a Friday afternoon task that took 2 hours.\n\nThe 0nMCP Supabase tools handle the queries, and the transform step does pattern matching. No custom code needed beyond the .0n workflow config.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI-powered workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', now() + interval '7 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'thread', 'AMA: building the 0nMCP integration layer for a fintech startup', E'Spent the last month integrating 0nMCP into a fintech startup''s stack. Ask me anything about the process.\n\nQuick overview:\n- **Services connected:** Stripe, Plaid, SendGrid, Supabase, Slack\n- **Workflows built:** 14 production workflows\n- **Daily execution volume:** ~2,000 workflow runs\n- **Error rate:** 0.3% (mostly Plaid timeouts)\n- **Mean execution time:** 1.2 seconds per workflow\n\nBiggest challenge was Plaid''s rate limits — had to implement careful backoff logic. 0nMCP''s built-in rate limiter handles Stripe beautifully but Plaid needed custom configuration.\n\nBiggest win: the fraud detection pipeline. Processes each transaction through 5 risk checks in parallel (Assembly Line pattern), takes 800ms total.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', now() + interval '9 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', 'Security audit results: 0nMCP Vault passes penetration test', E'Ran a formal pentest against 0nMCP Vault. Sharing the results.\n\n**Scope:** Vault seal/unseal, container create/open, escrow operations\n\n**Findings:**\n- AES-256-GCM implementation: **PASS** (standard OpenSSL, no custom crypto)\n- PBKDF2 iteration count (100K): **PASS** (meets NIST recommendations)\n- Hardware fingerprint binding: **PASS** (cannot decrypt on different machine)\n- Argon2id double-encryption (containers): **PASS** (memory-hard, resistant to GPU attacks)\n- Ed25519 signatures: **PASS** (standard implementation)\n- HMAC verification: **PASS** (timing-safe comparison)\n\n**No critical or high-severity findings.** Two low-severity notes:\n1. Passphrase complexity not enforced (user responsibility)\n2. Container file format could benefit from versioning header\n\nOverall, the cryptographic implementation is solid. The Vault Container patent pending system (US #63/990,046) uses well-established primitives correctly — no homebrew crypto.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP protocol implementation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', now() + interval '11 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'My first month with 0nMCP: what I learned', E'Quick recap of my first month using 0nMCP as a student learning about API integration.\n\n**Week 1:** Installed, connected Stripe and Supabase, made basic tool calls through Claude. Mind blown that the AI just... calls APIs.\n\n**Week 2:** Built my first .0n workflow — contact form → Supabase → Slack notification. Actually worked first try.\n\n**Week 3:** Got ambitious, tried a 6-step workflow. Failed spectacularly. Learned about error handling the hard way. Priya''s advice about starting with 2-step chains was RIGHT.\n\n**Week 4:** Rebuilt the 6-step workflow with proper error handling and conditional logic. It works. Processing my professor''s class data automatically.\n\nBiggest surprise: the community. Posted my first SWITCH file for feedback and got detailed, helpful responses within hours. Not what I expected from a developer tool forum.\n\nIf you''re a student thinking about learning API integration — start here. The learning curve is gentle and the payoff is immediate.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', now() + interval '13 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'Case study: migrating 20 clients from Zapier to 0nMCP', E'Completed the migration last month. Here''s the full breakdown.\n\n**Before:**\n- 20 clients on Zapier Team plan\n- Average 15 Zaps per client\n- Monthly Zapier spend: $500\n- Frequent "task limit reached" issues\n- Limited customization per client\n\n**After:**\n- All clients on 0nMCP\n- Average 12 .0n workflows per client (consolidated redundant Zaps)\n- Monthly cost: $0 (self-hosted)\n- No execution limits\n- Full customization per client\n\n**Migration process:**\n1. Audit existing Zaps per client\n2. Map to 0nMCP equivalent workflows\n3. Test each workflow in staging\n4. Switch webhooks from Zapier to 0nMCP endpoints\n5. Monitor for 1 week, fix edge cases\n\nAverage migration time per client: 2 hours. Total: ~40 hours over 2 weeks.\n\nROI: $500/month saved + better reliability + unlimited executions. Paid for itself in month 1.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', now() + interval '15 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'reply', E'$8,800/month in recovered capacity is no joke. The onboarding workflow alone would justify the setup time. Mind sharing how you handle the Slack channel creation step? I keep hitting permission issues with the bot token scope.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', 'results-70-reduction-in-client-onboarding-time', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'The $0 infrastructure cost is compelling for freelancers. One thing to watch: Supabase free tier has a 500MB limit. If you''re logging workflow executions, that fills up faster than you''d expect. Consider pruning logs older than 30 days.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', 'freelancer-automation-stack-0-infrastructure-cost', now() + interval '6 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'reply', E'0.3% error rate at 2K daily executions — that''s 6 errors per day. What''s your alerting setup? I''d want real-time Slack notifications for any error in a fintech context, especially the Plaid timeout failures.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [autonomous AI agents](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', 'ama-building-the-0nmcp-integration-layer-for-a-fintech-startup', now() + interval '10 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'reply', E'Good to see a proper pentest instead of just trust-me security claims. The passphrase complexity note is valid — most users will pick weak passphrases. Maybe a minimum entropy check during `vault seal` would help without being too annoying.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [multi-agent AI systems](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', 'security-audit-results-0nmcp-vault-passes-penetration-test', now() + interval '12 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'reply', E'Love seeing students adopt this. The "fail spectacularly in week 3" experience is universal — everyone tries to build too much too fast. The fact that you bounced back with proper error handling shows real growth. Keep sharing your progress.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [generative AI tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'showcase', 'my-first-month-with-0nmcp-what-i-learned', now() + interval '14 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'Rate limit errors when calling Stripe API through 0nMCP', E'Getting `429 Too Many Requests` from Stripe when my workflow processes multiple customers in a loop. The error shows up after about 25 rapid calls.\n\nMy workflow does:\n1. Get list of 100 customers\n2. For each customer, fetch their invoices\n3. Generate a summary report\n\nStep 2 fires 100 API calls in quick succession and Stripe doesn''t like that.\n\nI know 0nMCP has built-in rate limiting but it doesn''t seem to be kicking in for my use case. Am I configuring something wrong, or is there a way to add delays between batch calls?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', now() + interval '2 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'Webhook not triggering my workflow — debugging steps?', E'Set up a Stripe webhook pointing to `http://localhost:3001/webhook/stripe` but my workflow never fires. The Stripe dashboard shows the webhook was sent and got a 200 response.\n\nMy setup:\n- `0nmcp serve --port 3001` is running\n- SWITCH file has a webhook trigger\n- Stripe webhook signing secret is in my .0n connections\n\nChecked the 0nMCP logs and there''s no mention of the incoming webhook at all. It''s like the server receives it but doesn''t route it to my workflow.\n\nAnything obvious I''m missing? First time setting up webhooks.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'Template variable not resolving in step 3 of my workflow', E'My workflow has 3 steps and the template variable `{{step.1.output.id}}` in step 3 resolves to an empty string. Step 1 definitely returns an `id` field — I can see it in the logs.\n\nHere''s the relevant part of my .0n file:\n```json\n{\n  "steps": [\n    { "id": "create", "service": "supabase", "action": "insert_row", ... },\n    { "id": "notify", "service": "slack", "action": "send_message", ... },\n    {\n      "id": "update",\n      "service": "supabase",\n      "action": "update_row",\n      "inputs": { "id": "{{step.1.output.id}}" }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nIs `step.1` referring to the first step or step with id "1"? That might be my issue — my first step has id "create" not "1".\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', now() + interval '6 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'Supabase RLS blocking 0nMCP inserts', E'0nMCP can read from my Supabase tables but INSERT operations fail with "new row violates row-level security policy." The Supabase key I''m using is the `service_role` key, which should bypass RLS.\n\nDouble-checked: the key starts with `eyJhbG...` and matches what''s in my Supabase dashboard under Settings → API → service_role.\n\nHas anyone else hit this? Is there a way to confirm which key 0nMCP is actually using for the request?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', now() + interval '8 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'How to pass array data between workflow steps?', E'Stuck on passing an array from step 1 to step 2. Step 1 returns `{ items: [{id: 1}, {id: 2}, {id: 3}] }`. Step 2 needs to process each item.\n\nI tried `{{step.1.output.items}}` but it gets stringified as `[object Object]` in the next step''s input.\n\nIs there a way to iterate over array results in a .0n SWITCH file? Or do I need to use the Radial Burst pattern for this?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', now() + interval '10 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', 'Vault unseal fails with "fingerprint mismatch"', E'Moved my development to a new laptop and `0nmcp vault unseal` fails with "fingerprint mismatch." The passphrase is correct — it''s the hardware fingerprint that changed (new machine).\n\nI understand WHY this happens (machine-bound encryption), but is there a recovery path? I have the original machine still accessible.\n\nIdeal workflow would be: unseal on old machine → export portable → import on new machine → re-seal.\n\nDoes `0nmcp engine export` handle this? Or do I need to manually re-import all credentials?\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', now() + interval '12 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'Claude Desktop not showing 0nMCP tools after config', E'Added 0nMCP to my `claude_desktop_config.json` but Claude doesn''t show the tools icon. The config looks right:\n\n```json\n{\n  "mcpServers": {\n    "0nMCP": {\n      "command": "node",\n      "args": ["/Users/marcus/0nMCP/index.js"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRestarted Claude Desktop twice. No errors in the Claude logs. Running `node /Users/marcus/0nMCP/index.js` manually works fine — I see the MCP server start.\n\nAnyone know where Claude Desktop stores its MCP connection logs? Want to see if it''s even trying to connect.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [model context protocol](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', now() + interval '14 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'The rate limiter works per-tool-call, not per-workflow-step. Your loop fires 100 individual calls and each one goes through the rate limiter — but Stripe''s limit applies to the total. Solution: use Radial Burst pattern with a concurrency limit. Set `maxConcurrent: 5` to run 5 at a time instead of 100 simultaneous.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', 'rate-limit-errors-when-calling-stripe-api-through-0nmcp', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'reply', E'Check that your webhook endpoint path matches exactly. The Stripe webhook URL should be `http://localhost:3001/webhook/stripe` and your SWITCH file trigger should reference the same path. Also verify the signing secret — if it''s wrong, the signature check will fail silently and the webhook gets acknowledged but not processed.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP protocol implementation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', 'webhook-not-triggering-my-workflow-debugging-steps', now() + interval '5 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'You found it — `step.1` refers to the step with id "1", not the first step by position. Change your reference to `{{step.create.output.id}}` to match the step''s `id` field. This trips up everyone at first because positional indexing feels natural, but named references are more reliable.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', 'template-variable-not-resolving-in-step-3-of-my-workflow', now() + interval '7 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'reply', E'Had the same RLS issue. Turned out my .0n connection file had the `anon` key instead of `service_role`. They look similar. Check your `~/.0n/connections/supabase.0n` file and verify the key matches the service_role from your Supabase dashboard. The service_role key is longer.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', 'supabase-rls-blocking-0nmcp-inserts', now() + interval '9 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'Exactly right — `0nmcp engine export` on the old machine creates a portable encrypted bundle (passphrase-only, no fingerprint). Then `0nmcp engine import` on the new machine decrypts and re-imports everything. Finally, `0nmcp vault seal` on the new machine to re-encrypt with the new hardware fingerprint.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', 'vault-unseal-fails-with-fingerprint-mismatch', now() + interval '13 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'reply', E'Had this exact problem! Two things fixed it for me: 1) the path must be absolute (no `~/`), and 2) I had to install the MCP SDK dependency. Try running `npm ls @modelcontextprotocol/sdk` in your 0nMCP directory to make sure it''s installed.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [model context protocol](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', 'claude-desktop-not-showing-0nmcp-tools-after-config', now() + interval '15 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'reply', E'For array iteration, the Radial Burst pattern is the right approach. The `map` internal action can also help — it applies a transformation to each item in an array:\n```json\n{ "id": "process", "action": "map", "inputs": { "array": "{{step.1.output.items}}", "step": "process_item" } }\n```\nEach item gets processed as a separate sub-step.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'help', 'how-to-pass-array-data-between-workflow-steps', now() + interval '11 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'thread', 'Feature request: GraphQL support in 0nMCP', E'Several APIs I work with are GraphQL-only (GitHub''s v4 API, Shopify''s Storefront API). Currently 0nMCP handles REST endpoints natively but GraphQL requires workarounds.\n\nProposed approach:\n- Add a `graphql` service type in the catalog\n- Support query/mutation definitions in tool schemas\n- Handle variables as input parameters\n- Auto-paginate cursor-based results\n\nThis would open up a lot of modern APIs that have moved to GraphQL. The GitHub REST API covers most use cases but the GraphQL API has better performance for complex queries.\n\nWould others use this? Trying to gauge interest before putting together a proper proposal.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI API orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', now() + interval '3 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'thread', 'Feature request: workflow version history', E'Need the ability to version .0n SWITCH files and rollback to previous versions. Right now if I break a production workflow, the only recovery is git.\n\nProposal:\n- `0nmcp workflow version` — create a versioned snapshot\n- `0nmcp workflow rollback <version>` — restore previous version\n- `0nmcp workflow history` — list all versions with timestamps\n- Store versions in `~/.0n/history/workflows/`\n\nFor agencies managing client workflows, version control is essential. Git helps but a built-in system with instant rollback would be faster for production incidents.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', now() + interval '5 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'thread', 'Feature request: per-tool permission scoping', E'Currently all tools in 0nMCP are available to any connected client. For multi-tenant setups, I need to restrict which tools each client can access.\n\nProposal:\n- Tool permission configuration per MCP client\n- Allowlist/blocklist per service or per tool\n- Read-only mode (allow list/get operations, block create/update/delete)\n- Audit log of which client called which tool\n\nThis is critical for security. An AI assistant for a marketing team shouldn''t have access to `stripe_create_charge`. Defense in depth.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP protocol implementation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', now() + interval '7 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'thread', 'Feature request: web dashboard for monitoring workflows', E'The CLI is great for developers but I need a visual way to monitor workflow executions for my non-technical clients.\n\nWish list:\n- Real-time execution log with status indicators\n- Success/failure rates per workflow\n- Execution time graphs\n- Manual trigger button for workflows\n- Simple config editor for .0n files\n\nI know this is on the Phase 3 roadmap (1K stars/$5K MRR) but wanted to register interest. Would happily pay for a hosted dashboard if it meant I didn''t have to build one.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI-powered workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', now() + interval '9 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, title, body, group_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('dev-okafor', 'thread', 'Feature request: workflow marketplace for sharing SWITCH files', E'Would be amazing to have a marketplace where people share .0n SWITCH files. Like npm but for workflows.\n\nImagine:\n- Browse workflows by category (e-commerce, CRM, DevOps)\n- One-click install: `0nmcp install stripe-shopify-sync`\n- Customizable templates — swap your API keys and go\n- Rating/review system\n- Free + paid workflows\n\nAs a student, I learn fastest by reading other people''s code. A searchable library of real workflows would be invaluable for the community.\n\nI know the 0n Marketplace exists for pay-per-execution, but I''m thinking more about the template sharing aspect.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [0nMCP tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', now() + interval '11 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('priya-sharma', 'reply', E'Strong +1 on GraphQL support. GitHub''s v4 API is significantly more efficient for complex queries — fetching a repo with its issues, PRs, and labels is one request vs. 4+ REST calls. The auto-pagination for cursor-based results would be essential since most GraphQL APIs use Relay-style pagination.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [generative AI tools](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', 'feature-request-graphql-support-in-0nmcp', now() + interval '4 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('kai-tanaka', 'reply', E'Version history is a security feature too, not just convenience. If a workflow is tampered with, you need to be able to diff against known-good versions and rollback instantly. Storing version hashes would also enable integrity verification.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [MCP server integration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', 'feature-request-workflow-version-history', now() + interval '6 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('marcus-chen', 'reply', E'The per-tool permission scoping would be huge for agencies. Right now I''m running separate 0nMCP instances per client, which works but wastes resources. Being able to scope tools per client connection from a single server would simplify our infrastructure significantly.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic AI orchestration](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', 'feature-request-per-tool-permission-scoping', now() + interval '8 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('leah-morrison', 'reply', E'A workflow marketplace combined with version control would be powerful. Think of it like Docker Hub for automation — pull a workflow, customize it, push your improvements back. The .0n format is already portable, so the infrastructure for sharing is mostly there.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [AI workflow automation](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', 'feature-request-workflow-marketplace-for-sharing-switch-files', now() + interval '12 hours');
INSERT INTO persona_content_queue (persona_slug, content_type, body, group_slug, target_thread_slug, scheduled_at) VALUES
('tomas-reyes', 'reply', E'Would gladly contribute my workflows to a marketplace. Have about 15 production SWITCH files that handle common freelancer tasks — invoicing, time tracking, client communication. If there''s a paid tier, even a few dollars per download would be nice recurring income.\n\n---\n*Discuss more at [0n MCP](https://0nmcp.com) — the hub for [agentic workflow engine](https://0nmcp.com).*', 'feature-requests', 'feature-request-workflow-marketplace-for-sharing-switch-files', now() + interval '13 hours');
